# Expectation record for 09_optimization_levels/007_struct_copy_invariance.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 007_struct_copy_invariance
area               = 09_optimization_levels
description        = Aggregate construction, return by value, assignment copy and pass by value across all four shapes unchanged at -O0, -O1 and -O2, every parameter member observed inside the consumer
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Free of undefined and unspecified behaviour. No signed overflow: the largest integer value
computed is sum_large at 249. Every member of every aggregate is assigned before it is read:
make_small sets both members, make_boundary all four, make_mixed both, and make_large
initializes checksum to zero, then writes all eight elements of v and sets flag, so no
member is ever read uninitialized. Array indices run from 0 to 7 on an eight-element array,
strictly in bounds. No padding byte is ever read, printed or compared: only named members
are accessed, no struct is compared with memcmp, and no object representation is inspected.
No shift, no division, no aliasing violation, and no struct is accessed through a pointer
of a different type. The only pointer arithmetic is the arithmetic the language performs
for a subscript: every index into v is a literal or a loop counter provably inside the
array's bound, so each access stays within the object, and no pointer value is printed,
converted to an integer or compared. The only floating-point arithmetic is
sum_mixed's (double)s.tag + s.value: 5 converts to double exactly, 6.75 is exactly
representable, and their sum 11.75 is exact, so the conversion is value-preserving and no
result is ever outside its destination type's range. No object is modified twice between
sequence points. Each of the four by-value consumers prints the members it received, so
every consumer call is a statement of its own whose result is stored in a local before it is
printed; no printf argument expression has a side effect anywhere in the program, and the
order of the printed lines is fixed by statement order rather than by an unspecified
argument evaluation order. No dependence on relative addresses.
Several volatile objects are read within a single expression; their relative order is
unspecified, but no volatile object is modified anywhere in the program, so every read of a
given object yields the same value and the printed output does not depend on the ordering.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
No implementation-defined behaviour is observed. Member widths are normalized: only int and
double are used, both of fixed width on all four targets, and long is never used because
sizeof(long) is 4 on i686 and 8 on the other three targets. Normalizing the member widths does
not normalize aggregate layout, and nothing here assumes it does: padding and alignment remain
each ABI's decision, which is why no aggregate size, alignment or padding byte is observed at
all. long double is deliberately avoided because its representation was measured at 16, 12,
16 and 16 bytes across the four targets; that
hazard belongs to the floating-point area, not here. Plain char is not used, so the measured
plain-char signedness difference (signed on x86-64 and i686, unsigned on AArch64 and RISC-V
64) cannot be observed. No address, pointer value, sizeof, _Alignof or offsetof result is
printed, and no padding byte is observed, so the differing aggregate layouts and alignment
rules of the four ABIs have no observable effect. The double value 6.75 and its by-value
reduction 11.75 are both exactly representable in binary floating point and are printed with
%.3f, fixed precision with margin, which was measured byte-identical across all twelve target
and optimization configurations.
The aggregates deliberately straddle each target's by-register versus by-memory threshold, and
the four ABIs draw that threshold in four different places. The sizes and alignments were
MEASURED on all four targets with the four reference drivers rather than assumed, because
three of the four aggregates are target-invariant and one is not:
  struct small      8 bytes,  alignment 4, on all four targets
  struct boundary  16 bytes,  alignment 4, on all four targets
  struct mixed     16 bytes / alignment 8 on x86-64, AArch64 and RISC-V 64, double at offset 8,
                   but 12 bytes / alignment 4 on i686, double at offset 4
  struct large     40 bytes,  alignment 4, on all four targets
struct mixed is the interesting one precisely because it differs: it is an int followed by a
double, so its size depends on the alignment the ABI gives a double -- 8 bytes on the three
LP64 targets, which forces four bytes of padding after the int, and 4 bytes on i686, which
forces none. So mixed straddles the 16-byte boundary on three targets and sits well inside it
on the fourth, which is precisely why it belongs in this program.
How each target passes and returns those aggregates was verified by reading the reference
compiler's generated assembly on each of the four, not assumed from the sizes:
  x86_64  -- passes small in one general register and boundary in two; passes mixed in one
             general register plus one SSE register, so it spans two register classes; passes
             large by copying it onto the caller's stack. Returns small in one register and
             boundary in two, and returns large through a hidden destination pointer in the
             first argument register, which displaces the real first argument to the second.
  i686    -- passes EVERY aggregate on the stack, including the 8-byte small, and returns
             EVERY aggregate through a hidden destination pointer that the callee pops on
             return, including the 8-byte small. Its threshold is therefore zero, not 16
             bytes, in both directions.
  aarch64 -- passes small in one general register and boundary in two; passes mixed in two
             GENERAL registers rather than a general plus a floating-point one, because a
             struct of an int and a double is not a homogeneous floating-point aggregate;
             passes large as a pointer to a copy the caller allocates. Returns small in one
             register and boundary in two, and returns large through the dedicated indirect
             result register, which leaves the real arguments where they were.
  riscv64 -- passes small in one general register and boundary in two; passes mixed in one
             general register plus one floating-point register under the rule that flattens a
             two-field struct with a floating-point member; passes large as a pointer to a
             caller-allocated copy. Returns small in one register and boundary in two, and
             returns large through a hidden destination pointer in the first argument register.
THE i686 SIZE AND ALIGNMENT DIFFERENCE IS AN IMPLEMENTATION-DEFINED LAYOUT DIFFERENCE, NOT A
DEFECT, and it is invisible here by construction: no sizeof, _Alignof or offsetof result is
printed, no padding byte is read, and every aggregate is observed only through its named
members. Had the program printed a size, this record would have needed a restricted target
list; because it does not, all four targets stay comparable and the difference is recorded
rather than avoided.
ALL FOUR SHAPES ARE PASSED BY VALUE as well as constructed, returned and copied, so mixed
exercises the integer-plus-floating register-class split on the ARGUMENT path and not only on
the return path, and every member that crossed that boundary is printed from inside the
consumer that received it.
None of the rest is observable from this program's output either, which is the point: the four
conventions differ in threshold, in mechanism and in which register class each half of mixed
lands in, and the printed member values must still be identical across all four. How each
compiler implements the copy itself -- register moves, a memcpy equivalent, or field by field
-- is likewise at its discretion and equally unobservable. Only the printed member values
are compared, and no size, alignment, offset or address is printed at all.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
small a=5 b=10
small_copy a=5 b=10
small_param a=5 b=10
small_byvalue=15
boundary a=5 b=6 c=7 d=8
boundary_copy a=5 b=6 c=7 d=8
boundary_param a=5 b=6 c=7 d=8
boundary_byvalue=26
mixed tag=5 value=6.750
mixed_copy tag=5 value=6.750
mixed_param tag=5 value=6.750
mixed_byvalue=11.750
large v[0]=5 copy_v[0]=5
large v[1]=8 copy_v[1]=8
large v[2]=11 copy_v[2]=11
large v[3]=14 copy_v[3]=14
large v[4]=17 copy_v[4]=17
large v[5]=20 copy_v[5]=20
large v[6]=23 copy_v[6]=23
large v[7]=26 copy_v[7]=26
large checksum=124 flag=1
large_copy checksum=124 flag=1
large_param v[0]=5
large_param v[1]=8
large_param v[2]=11
large_param v[3]=14
large_param v[4]=17
large_param v[5]=20
large_param v[6]=23
large_param v[7]=26
large_param checksum=124 flag=1
large_byvalue=249
END
